    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-Professor---Medieval-and-or-transregional-history--7th-16th-centuries_JR38632-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Medieval and/or transregional history, 7th-16th centuries",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: Department of History_Professors and Researchers Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/26 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): The Department of History at the University of Ottawa invites applications for a position in medieval and/or transregional history, 7th-16th centuries . This tenure-track appointment will be made at the assistant professor level, beginning July 1, 2027 .   The University of Ottawa is recognized nationally and internationally as a leading university and the largest bilingual university (French and English) in the world. Located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe-Algonquin people, in the heart of downtown Ottawa, it contributes significantly to the economy, culture and quality of life of Canada’s capital.   Position Title: Assistant Professor.   Duties: Responsibilities include, in varying proportions: a) active research and knowledge mobilization program; b) teaching; c) recruitment and supervision of graduate students; d) service to the community. ·        Maintain and develop a research program in medieval and/or transregional history, 7th-16th centuries . ·        Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in medieval and/or transregional history, 7th-16th centuries in both French and English. ·        Recruit and train graduate students. ·        Work collaboratively in research and service. ·        Participate in administrative activities of the department.   Terms: New tenure-track position.   Salary: The starting salary for an Assistant Professor is $99,377 (according to the scale in effect as of May 1, 2025).   Benefits Package: The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package , which includes long-term disability insurance, basic group life insurance, health insurance, University of Ottawa pension plan and optional life insurance, as well as reimbursement of eligible relocation expenses.   Work location: Department of History, University of Ottawa, 55 Laurier Avenue East, Ottawa, ON K1N 6N5.   Contact Information (for inquiries): Dr. Eric Allina, Director, Department of History, University of Ottawa, 55 Laurier Avenue East, Ottawa ON, K1N 6N5. E-mail: HISdir@uottawa.ca   Required Qualifications: The successful candidate will be a scholar in medieval and/or transregional history, 7th-16th centuries , with the following: ·        Possess a doctorate (Ph.D.) by July 1, 2027, in History or a related discipline. ·        A demonstrated track record of research excellence, including peer-reviewed publications, in medieval and/or transregional history, 7th-16th centuries . ·        Excellent communication skills in either English or French. English-French bilingualism is an asset. Active knowledge of Canada’s two official languages (French and English) is a requirement for tenure. ·        Evidence of excellence in the teaching of history or of a related discipline. ·        Experience attracting and supervising graduate students is an asset. ·        Competence in creative design of user experiences to mobilize historical knowledge in an online environment is an asset. ·        Ability to train students to engage the profound changes produced by digital technologies and create, use and deploy digital tools for critical analysis of theories, models and methods in digital humanities is an asset.   Deadline: Applications received by October 16, 2026 will receive full consideration but the position will remain open until filled. More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online . Complete application must be submitted via the University of Ottawa Academic Careers application portal.   Please submit the following application materials as a single PDF document: cover letter, curriculum vitae, two recent peer reviewed publications, statement of research interests, and an example of a course syllabus. Following an initial review of applications, selected candidates will be invited to present their research to professors and students and asked to have three confidential letters of reference submitted directly by the referees. The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application.   All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada   The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence.   The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language.   Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-professor---Urban-Sustainability_JR38643-1": {
      "title": "Assistant professor - Urban Sustainability",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: Department of Geography, Environment and Geomatics_Professors and Researchers Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/24 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): The Department of Geography, Environment and Geomatics at the University of Ottawa invites application for a position in urban sustainability . This tenure-track appointment will be made at the assistant professor level, beginning July 1, 2027 . The position will primarily complement and contribute to the Department’s expertise in human geography. The Department seeks a scholar whose research and teaching examine urban environmental change, with an emphasis on social, political and/or economic responses to the challenge of building resilient cities. Position title: Assistant Professor The Department is particularly interested in candidates whose research examines any of the following: The environmental and social implications of contemporary urban form and development; Urban governance and political ecology; Housing, affordability and climate adaptation; Transportation, mobility and urban infrastructure; Community & neighbourhood mobilization in response to environmental change and evolving urban landscapes; Technological innovations and sustainability transitions; Energy systems, built environments and social resilience; Approaches to social equity and urban sustainability. The University of Ottawa is recognized nationally and internationally as a leading university and the largest bilingual university (French and English) in the world. Located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe-Algonquin people, in the heart of downtown Ottawa, it contributes significantly to the economy, culture, and quality of life in Canada’s capital. The start date is expected to be July 1, 2027. Duties: The functions of a member of the academic staff include, in varying proportions: a) teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in both French and English; b) scholarly activities, research and knowledge mobilization; c) academic service and administration of the department; d) recruitment and supervision of students. A commitment to building the discipline and academic unit, including graduate programming, is required. To this end, the successful candidate must establish a rigorous research program that includes grants from major funding agencies such as SSHRC or a clearly defined plan to develop an active research program. The successful candidate will be expected to teach at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, in English and French. Terms: New Tenure-track position Salary: The starting salary for an Assistant Professor is $99,377 (according to the scale in effect as of May 1, 2025). Benefits package: Thе University оf Ottаwа prоvides an ехtensive benefits paсkage , which inсludеs lоng-tеrm disability cоverage, basic life insurance, supplеmеntary hеаlth bеnefits, a pensiоn plаn, оptiоnal lifе insurance, and rеimbursement fоr rеlосatiоn eхpenses. Work location: Department of Geography, Environment and Geomatics , University of Ottawa, 60 University Private, Ottawa, ON, K1N 6N5. Contact Information (for inquiries): Please address questions about this position to Dr. Anders Knudby, Director, Department of Geography, Environment and Geomatics , University of Ottawa, 60 University Private, Ottawa, ON, K1N 6N5. E-mail: gegdir@uottawa.ca Required qualifications: Possess a doctorate (Ph.D.) by July 1, 2027 in Geography or a related discipline; Candidates must have excellent communication skills in English or French. English-French bilingualism is an asset. Active knowledge of Canada’s two official languages (French and English) is a requirement for tenure; Evidence of an established and innovative research program and a record of refereed publications and scholarly conferences; An ability to 