    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Avocate-gnrale--avocat-gnral_JR40206": {
      "title": "General Counsel",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Job Family: Administrative Upper Management Faculty/Service - Department: Office of the Secretary General Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/15 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/06 Position Purpose: Reporting to the Secretary-General, the General Counsel is the University’s principal legal officer and most senior legal advisor. The General Counsel provides trusted, strategic and solution-oriented counsel to the President, Secretary-General, Vice-Presidents, senior leaders and other University decision-makers on sensitive, high-stakes matters. The role safeguards the University’s legal interests, reputation and public mission while enabling sound, timely and well-informed decision-making. As head of the Legal Counsel Office, the General Counsel sets the strategic direction for the University’s legal services, leads and develops the internal legal team, and oversees the effective use of external counsel. The incumbent promotes a proactive, coordinated and service-oriented approach to legal risk, regulatory compliance, dispute prevention and institutional governance. The portfolio spans the full range of legal issues affecting a large, bilingual post-secondary institution, including governance, administrative law, corporate and commercial matters, policy development and interpretation, charities, procurement, intellectual property, access to information and privacy, contracts, estates and trusts, construction, information technology, real estate, insurance, employment and labour relations, human rights, litigation and dispute resolution.   In this role, your responsibilities will include: Provides timely, practical and strategic legal advice to the President, Secretary-General, Vice-Presidents and senior leaders on significant institutional matters. Leads the Legal Counsel Office, establishes service priorities and standards, allocates resources, and supervises, mentors and develops legal and administrative staff. Anticipates, assesses and communicates legal, regulatory, operational and reputational risks, and recommends proportionate strategies that protect the University while advancing its academic and administrative objectives. Provides legal and strategic guidance on institutional governance, decision-making authorities, policies, procedures and regulatory obligations, promoting defensible and transparent practices. Oversees the drafting, negotiation, review and interpretation of contracts, agreements, settlements, pleadings, tribunal materials and other legal instruments involving significant institutional interests. Directs the University’s response to litigation, claims, investigations, regulatory matters and disputes; develops resolution strategies and ensures senior leaders are appropriately briefed. Selects, retains and instructs external counsel and other legal service providers; manages performance, costs and outcomes to ensure high-quality and cost-effective support. Monitors legislative, regulatory and common-law developments affecting the University and translates emerging requirements into practical compliance, policy and operational responses. Builds strong, trusted relationships across the University and promotes early consultation, coordinated legal services and effective collaboration among specialized functions. Strengthens institutional legal capability through education, tools, templates and training that support informed decision-making and consistent risk management. Undertakes other strategic or legal responsibilities assigned by the Secretary-General. What you will bring: LL.B. or J.D., with membership in good standing in the Law Society of Ontario. At least 12 years of progressively responsible legal practice, including substantial experience as a General Counsel, senior in-house counsel or legal leader in a university, public-sector or comparably multifaceted organization. Excellent oral and written communication skills in both English and French. Demonstrated success leading an in-house legal function, managing professional staff, setting priorities and overseeing external counsel and legal budgets. Broad expertise across the principal areas of practice relevant to the University, with the judgment and agility to address unfamiliar or rapidly evolving issues. Superior strategic judgment, legal analysis, negotiation, advocacy, drafting and presentation skills, with a record of providing clear, practical and defensible advice on high-stakes matters. Strong understanding of governance, administrative decision-making, regulatory compliance and enterprise risk in a large, multi-stakeholder institution. Strong business acumen and demonstrated comfort with complex transactional matters or files across multiple practice areas. Exceptional relationship-building and influencing skills, including the ability to reconcile divergent perspectives, foster consensus and maintain trust in sensitive or contentious situations. Highly developed organizational skills, with the ability to prioritize competing demands, lead through ambiguity and respond calmly and effectively in a crisis. Impeccable integrity, discretion, independence of judgment and commitment to professional ethics. *This position may be subject to a telework agreement. #LI-Hybrid The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Supervisor--Communications-and-Marketing--University-of-Ottawa-Press_JR40271": {
      "title": "Supervisor, Communications and Marketing, University of Ottawa Press",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: New Position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 24 Job Family: Communications and Marketing (JF) # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Research, Communication & Marketing Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/15 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/26 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 11 Salary Range: $95,594.00 - $120,750.00 Position purpose: Reporting to the Director, the incumbent plays a leadership role in developing and implementing strategic, branded, content-based marketing, sales, and communications plans and budgets to showcase PUO-UOP’s bilingual publishing program of 25-30 titles annually, with a backlist of 1,500 active titles (print, digital, audio), to achieve revenue goals, enhance the PUO-UOP brand, and bolster the University’s reputation, locally, nationally, and internationally. This person develops and deploys thoughtful, curated, data-driven marketing campaigns designed to stand out in today’s book industry; develops dynamic events programming and knowledge mobilization initiatives; fosters strong media relations within mainstream media as well as in specialized fields; fosters strong public relations to grow our community of readers, authors, reviewers, book professionals, and other stakeholders; identifies and secures partnerships to further promotional efforts; creates exciting print and online advertising and promotion pieces, superior copy and metadata, and strong awards submissions. In this role, your responsibilities will include: Develop the annual and long-term strategic and integrated sales, marketing, and communications plans for our scholarly and trade lists and oversees its implementation by the Marketing team consisting of one FTE, sales and marketing agencies, and various designers, writers and translators, and others suppliers. These plans are designed to showcase Press publications and achieve, or exceed, revenue goals, foster engagement, build networks of customers, readers and booksellers, and enhance the reputation of the Press and its authors, as well as that of the University of Ottawa. Develop the annual marketing budget, which comprises print and online advertising; website hosting, updates, and refreshes; a prolific events program; two seasonal catalogues and other marketing collaterals; and other marketing-related expenses designed to enhance visibility and sales. Monitors the budget and makes necessary adjustments throughout the year. Manage all aspects of domestic and international marketing and sales for 25-30 new titles and a backlist of approximately 1,500 active titles, including advertising, promotions, publicity, print and digital marketing materials, galleys, awards, events, and exhibits. Solicits and manages sponsorships, partnerships, media coverage and reviews. What you will bring: University degree in marketing, communications, or related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience Minimum of 5 years of experience (equivalent full-time) in marketing and communications, including media/public relations, events coordination, or advertising and publicity Excellent organizational skills and time management skills, to see various marketing and communications projects through to completion, working under pressure and around strict deadlines, budgets, and multiple priorities; Bilingualism: fluency in French and English (spoken and written), with superior writing and editing skills in both languages Superior public relations, interpersonal, communication, and negotiation skills to engage with diverse groups (clients, authors, faculty, senior management, media, funders, sales teams, partners, etc.); Demonstrated ability to develop strategic, metrics-based marketing and sales planning and execution Superior problem-solving and conflict resolution skills Team player with demonstrated commitment to the value of diversity, equity, and inclusion initiatives Demonstrated ability to work independently and with minimal supervision Strong technical skills, including Microsoft Office, Google Suite, and database management Experience working with confidential information Ability to travel as needed to professional conferences and to meet with faculty at other campuses Ability to work flexible hours on occasion, including evenings and weekends *This position may be eligible for a telework arrangement. Learn more about our telework guidelines click here #LI-Hybrid Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:  Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Lead-Coordinator--Event-Management_JR40128": {
      "title": "Lead Coordinator, Event Management",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Temporary replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 16 Job Family: Event Management # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Campus: Desmarais Hall Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/15 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/26 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 09 Salary Range: $76,104.00 - $96,135.00 About the Telfer School of Management The Telfer School of Management at the University of Ottawa is located in the heart of Ottawa and is the proud academic home of some 4,300 students,200 faculty members, over 100 staff members, and over 30,000 alumni. At Telfer, everything we do from teaching to research, to policy advice is firmly aligned with helping Canada reach its potential. We believe that better business practice means a better Canada and that a better Canada means a better world. Position purpose Plans, manages and promotes events that engage alumni, raise awareness in the community, foster ties between strategic partners and the academic community, and support recruitment, fundraising and research activities. In this role, you will: Event planning: Plan and design, for the entire faculty or service, various on- and off-campus events that engage alumni, raise awareness in the community, build ties between strategic partners and the academic community, and support recruitment, fundraising and research activities. To this end, discusses with clients, identifies requirements and deadlines, set budgets, plans and coordinate all the steps required to conduct the event. Event Management: Organize and manage all the logistics surrounding events, such as reservations, rentals, registration and invitations, catering services, equipment installation and set up of spaces. Supervise the running of these events and remain available to resolve any problems that might arise. Welcome guests and acts as University representative and host for each event. Supervision: Hire and train individuals, such as volunteers and caterers, and coordinate and supervise their work to ensure that they carry out their duties as directed and to the required standard. Budget Management: Manage budgets related to the organization and running of events, while staying within budget limits and complying with related policies, regulations and procedures. Advertising: Participate in planning and designing the marketing plans required to conduct events and maximize stakeholder awareness and participation. Quality assurance: Develop performance indicators and monitor performance by conducting consultations and surveys in order to maximize event effectiveness. Analyze results and make improvements to optimize event protocols. Write reports, summaries and post-event evaluations to ensure that each event has met its objectives. Communication and liaison: Establish and maintain effective working relationships with suppliers and with internal and external partners to ensure that the events for which the incumbent is responsible run smoothly. What you will bring College diploma in marketing, event management, fundraising, or communications, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience Minimum five years’ experience in a similar role Proven ability to formulate creative ideas and to analyze, evaluate and negotiate details to create a successful event Strong interpersonal and communication skills Excellent problem-solving skills Ability to work well under pressure and meet strict deadlines Self-reliance and good judgment Project management experience Experience in supervising teams and volunteers Knowledge of budget management policies and practices Strong organizational skills Knowledge of computer systems and software such as Windows, word processors, web applications and graphic design software, social media platforms, spreadsheets, databases, presentation software, internet browsers and email Bilingualism – French and English (spoken and written) Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:  Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Research-Assistant_JR40270-1": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location where work is to be performed: Main Campus Session: 2026 Fall Semester | Trimestre d'automne Faculty: Faculté des sciences sociales / Faculty of Social Sciences Unit: Department of Criminology_Cheryl Webster_JM Job Classification: Research Assistant (CUPE) Posting Type: Recherche / Research Course Title: N/A Course Code: N/A Section: Supervisor name (if known): Cheryl Webster Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/15 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/28 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Préparation / Preparation:0 Contact avec étudiants / Contact with Students:0 Correction - Notation / Grading:0 Surveillance / Proctoring:0 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 1 Expected Enrolment: N/A Work Start Date: octobre 29, 2026 Work End Date: décembre 22, 2026 Total Work Hours: 120 Hourly Rate: AE-RA-Tuteur / TA-RA-Tutor : $33.83 ou/or $53.31 (Sept. 2026 - Aug. 2027) Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: N/A Language of Work: Anglais | English Requirements and Nature of Work: Responsibilities: 1 – conduct an extensive literature review of the national and international research, policy and programs on the processing of cases in Bail Court.   2 - carry out 50-60 hours of bail court observations in both video remand court and show cause hearings in the Ottawa Courthouse (161 Elgin Street, Ottawa).   3 – code preliminary quantitative and qualitative data on the processing as well as the outcomes of observed bail cases. Qualifications : 1 - Graduate-level knowledge of the operation of bail court in Canada, as well as the relevant legislative, judicial, policy and scholarly research/literature on the topic   2 - a minimum of 75-100 hours of bail court observations conducted in an Ontario bail court   3 - direct experience in the collection, coding and analyses of quantitative and qualitative data on the routine processing of bail court cases (including both first appearance cases and show cause hearings)   4 - general knowledge of international trends and practices   5 - experience with SPSS/STATA. Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Lead-Coordinator--Academic-Administration--Psychedelics-and-Consciousness-Studies-_JR40268": {
      "title": "Lead Coordinator, Academic Administration (Psychedelics and Consciousness Studies)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: New Position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): N/A Job Family: Academic Administration # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Operations_School of Psychology Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/15 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/26 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 09 Salary Range: $76 104,00 - $96 135,00 Job Description Summary About the Faculty of Social Sciences The FSS comprises nine departments, schools and institutes, which offer undergraduate, masters and doctoral programs in both English and French. With its 10,000 students, 260 full-time professors, and wide array of programs and research centres, the Faculty of Social Sciences plays a key role at the heart of the University of Ottawa. Its graduate students are supervised by excellent researchers and undertake cutting-edge research in the Faculty's masters and Ph.D. programs. Along with its complement of about 100 administrative employees, the faculty is proud to provide a collaborative working environment that inspires new ideas, encourages creative ingenuity, and promotes excellence. Position Purpose Administers, coordinates and evaluates the operational delivery and continuous improvement of specialized programs within the School of Psychology.  Develops, implements and maintains administrative processes and communication strategies that support program objectives and ensure efficient service delivery.  Fosters effective stakeholder engagement and ensures compliance with institutional policies, regulatory requirements and established standards.  Provides operational coordination for day-to-day program activities, supports program leadership in planning and decision-making, and contributes to the ongoing success, quality and growth of the School’s specialized program offerings.  In this role, your responsibilities will include: Operations and Program Administration: Leads and coordinates administrative operations that support the effective delivery of program activities and initiatives.  Develops, implements and continuously improves workflows, procedures and operational practices to enhance efficiency, service quality and student experience.  Supports the development and implementation of new programs, partnerships, and strategic initiatives.  Serves as a resource to internal and external stakeholders on program requirements and processes and ensures that operational activities comply with institutional policies, program objectives and applicable regulatory requirements.  Student Services and Academic Administration: Advises and supports students, faculty and staff regarding academic policies, procedures and program requirements.  Assesses student needs, interprets and applies academic regulations and resolves complex situations requirement sound judgement and policy application.  Coordinates academic administrative processes and records management, collaborates with faculty and administrative partners to support student success and recommends improvement to services, procedures and practices that enhance the student experience. Stakeholder Engagement and Relationship Management: Develops, fosters and maintains effective working relationships with internal and external stakeholders and partners to support the planning, delivery and continuous improvement of program activities. Coordinates stakeholder communications, facilitates meetings and consultations and serves as a liaison between program leadership and partners. Exercises a high degree of diplomacy, discretion and judgement when handling sensitive or confidential information. Communication and Information Management: Develops, coordinates and evaluates communication strategies and information sharing activities that support program goals and stakeholder engagement.  Creates and maintains communication material, in collaboration with the Faculty’s marketing and communications team.  Ensures communications are accurate, accessible and appropriate to diverse audiences.  Supports marketing, outreach, recruitment and engagement activities.  Monitors the effectiveness of communication activities, recommending enhancements as required. Regulatory Compliance and Risk Management: Monitors and supports compliance with institutional, professional, ethical and regulatory requirements governing program activities.  Ensures adherence to applicable policies, procedures and operational standards.  Maintains records and documentation required for audits, accreditation, quality assurance and reporting purposes.  Identifies operational risks and recommends mitigation strategies.  Supports the implementation of changes arising from the evolving regulations, policies and organizational priorities.  Financial and Resources Coordination: In collaboration with the Faculty’s finance team, coordinates and monitors financial and administrative processes that support program operations and resource management.  Tracks expenditures and budget activities.  Coordinates various financial and HR processes with the designated teams within the Faculty.  Ensures all financial activities comply with institutional policies, procedures and accountability requirements. Project Coordination and Administration: Coordinates and oversees the implementation of projects, special initiatives and program enhancements within the School of Psychology.  Maintains project plans, timelines, documentation, records and performance metrics. Coordinates activities among internal and external stakeholders.  Identifies and resolves operational challenges and ensures projects and program activities are delivered efficiently, consistently and in alignment with established objectives timelines and quality standards. Event and Educational Activity Coordination: Plans and coordinates educational professional development and community engagement activities in support of program objectives.  Oversees all event logistics in support of the delivery of workshops, seminars, webinars, conferences and special events.  Evaluates outcomes and participant feedback and recommends improvements to ensure high-quality and engaging participant experience.  What you will bring: Undergraduate degree in public administration, business administration or a related field Minimum three years of progressive experience in program and academic administration Strong problem-solving skills and analytical ability Ability to make and implement decisions quickly Experience in coordinating multiple projects and competing priorities Experience interpreting and applying policies, procedures and regulations Experience developing reports and programming documentation Able to facilitate effective and diverse community consultations, programs, and initiatives Demonstrated ability to relate to and respect students and faculty with diverse backgrounds, experiences and identities in ways that build trust and confidence while meeting their unique needs Discretion, confidentiality, tact and diplomacy Strong communication skills, including experience with event organization, social media and creating content required to reach different stakeholder audiences Experience in an advising role and in customer service in an academic setting Able to work both independently Organizational and interpersonal skills, as well as good judgment Experience using computer systems (uoCampus, Talisma, Calculator, Document Manager, or similar systems) and applications and platforms such as Windows, word processing software, databases, spreadsheets, email and the Internet Bilingualism – French and English (spoken and written) Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:  Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Analyste-intermdaire--Assurance-de-la-qualit-des-TI--Projet-de-CRM-Agora_JR39716": {
      "title": "Intermediate Analyst, IT Quality Assurance, Agora CRM Project",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: New Position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 18 Job Family: Quality Assurance # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Research & Pipeline Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: UOITP Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/15 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/26 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: UOITP Grade 10 Salary Range: $82 450,00 - $102 514,00 University Advancement plays a pivotal role in fostering meaningful connections between the University and its alumni, donors, and partners. Our team is dedicated to advancing the University's mission by securing philanthropic support, engaging with our vibrant alumni community, and building partnerships that drive innovation and excellence. Through strategic initiatives and collaborative efforts, we help shape the future of the University, ensuring that it continues to thrive as a leading institution of higher education and research. Together, we empower our students, faculty and researchers to make a lasting impact on the world. Position Purpose: The Quality Assurance Analyst ensures quality throughout the software development process while working on projects to deliver new computer application and modifications existing computer applications. The incumbent reviews project deliverables, design documents, and programming specification to verify quality standards. This role contributes to the formulation and continuous improvement of software quality standards, methods, and tools for all software development and maintenance projects carried out within the department. For the Advancement CRM project, the role supports quality assurance activities related to CRM functionality, integration, data migration, security, and business processes. In this role, your responsibilities will include: Testing: Prepares specific business test cases. Reviews, and updates, develops and executes test cases and test scripts, including those related to CRM functionality, integrations, regression, data migration and user acceptance testing, to fully assess if the solution meets the requirements presented according to existing test plans. Records results. Ensures test cases sufficiently cover all areas of business need and application functionality.   Development: Ensures quality throughout the software development process; proper quality checkpoints. Has authority to reject documents that do not meet standards. Develops complete test strategies for new software products and confirm testing before production. Verification: Organizes activities designed to verify system requirements, system design and system code, including security roles and permissions, business process workflows and integrations. Ensures the test plan will validate the application requirements are met. Develops strategies for client acceptance. Logging: Finds, reproduces, isolates, logs, and reports defects as needed including defects related to integrations, data conversions and business process execution. Works closely with the development team(s) during the test design and test execution phases. Plans, designs, develops, implements and maintains processes for manual testing. Ensures that problems are logged quickly and accurately, and that \"fixed\" problems are verified and closed.  Project Management: In partnership with a project manager or senior programmer takes full responsibility for software testing activities and coordinates testing with technical and business stakeholders and to deliver new applications and modifications to existing applications. Plans project time requirements, resources requirements, and risk analysis.  Risk Analysis: Evaluates risks, in the preparation of the test plan and as the test execution progresses. Recommends risk assessment and confirms launch readiness for projects with substantial visibility or impact. Implementation: Conducts post-implementation reviews and to evaluate the software development process and make recommendation for improvement. Documentation: Produces all documentation of the testing process, validation of software, strategies and best practices. Records defects and reviews them with the project manager, and will contribute a defect analysis to the project closeout document. Production Control : Periodically monitor all production jobs on an on-call basis after hours; in case of production failures identify the nature of the failure, analyze the impact of the failed processes, initiate recovery procedure, restore files and resubmit jobs. Troubleshoot production errors and coordinate with clients, application managers and vendors when required. Ensure formal documentation of production and change control processes is followed. Data Security : Responsible for ensuring confidentiality, security and accessibility of institutional data. The incumbent complies with and promotes any controls put in place to protect and secure data and exhibits due diligence in following the established information security and privacy policies as well as related standards. What you will bring: University degree in computer science or a related discipline or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Minimum 5 years of experience in a quality assurance role involving software testing. Bilingualism – French and English (spoken and written). ISTQB or equivalent QA certification is an asset. Familiarity with advancement/fundraising processes is an asset. Experience testing Salesforce implementations from requirements through deployment. Experience testing complex Salesforce business processes that span multiple user roles and integrate with external systems. Experience testing Salesforce security, including roles, profiles, and permission sets. Experience creating and executing functional, regression, integration, data migration, API, and performance tests. Experience developing and maintaining automated UI tests using tools such as Playwright. Experience with API testing tools such as Postman or Bruno. Experience using Jira and Xray (or equivalent) for test management, defect tracking, traceability, execution, and reporting. Experience working with developers, business analysts, Salesforce administrators, and business stakeholders in an Agile or project delivery environment. Strong test planning and test case design/documentation skills. Experience with performance testing tools such as JMeter or Artillery. Experience testing data migration/data quality in a CRM handling sensitive donor or financial data. Experience testing Education Cloud. Ability to communicate testing outcomes to non-technical stakeholders. *This position may be subject to a telework agreement. #LI-Hybrid Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:  Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Coordinator--Academic-Program-Management_JR40244": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Academic Program Management",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Replacement of a temporary position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 23 Job Family: Academic Program Management # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: DFM Education Campus: Roger Guindon Hall Union Affiliation: N/A Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/15 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/26 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: Non-Union Grade NC3 - 35 hrs Salary Range: $58,441.00 - $73,051.00 About the Faculty of Medicine Welcome to the University of Ottawa Faculty of Medicine, the first and largest bilingual medical faculty in Canada. Our learners have access to state-of-the art facilities and specialized institutions as they learn alongside world-class physicians and scientists. Consistently ranked among the top 10 medical schools in Canada, our faculty is among the top faculties of medicine in the world for research impact in biomedical and health sciences. Position Purpose: Under the direction of the Postgraduate Education Manager, the Clinical Administrator is responsible for providing administrative support to the Civic Postgraduate Manager, the Unit Director and other key faculty to ensure that residents and faculty have access to high quality, timely educational programming in support of the Family Medicine Academic Program.  In this role, your responsibilities will include: Schedules all staff physicians and resident obligations to ensure that all academic and clinical requirements are met and updated while taking into consideration all personal and academic obligations of each faculty member and resident; this includes coordination of call schedules, clinical schedules, and teaching schedules. Maintains an up-to-date record of all leave that has been taken by each faculty member and resident and makes appropriate changes to all schedules affected to ensure efficiency and effectiveness of the program. Assists in meeting preparation, minutes taking, event planning (CaRMS, Graduation, etc.), typing of correspondence for ongoing sustainability of administration within the program. Maintains current record of term contracts or invoices, all shifts worked by contracted or casual staff (locums) and submits remittance advice to the accountant on a monthly basis to ensure that all locums are paid. What you will bring: Postsecondary education or equivalent Minimum of 3 years of experience in an administrative role Experience working within a medical and educational environment would be an asset Knowledge and experience of administrative and office procedures Knowledge of a complex scheduling system Knowledge of computer software such as e-mail (outlook), internet, MS Suite Ability to work under pressure Ability to work independently Able to work alone on a broad variety of projects Ability to maintain a High degree of judgment, sensitivity, initiative, assertiveness, courtesy, and diplomacy in dealing with people on a daily basis Able to establish and maintain healthy working relationships with people in course of work Excellent organizational skills Excellent oral and written communication skills in English. French is considered an asset. Ability to maintain a high degree of confidentiality, professionalism and dependability Flexibility #LI-Hybrid Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:  Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Director--Human-Rights-Office_JR39952": {
      "title": "Director, Human Rights Office",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Job Family: Student and Campus Support Faculty/Service - Department: Office of the Secretary General Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/15 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/26 Salary Grade: Non-Union Grade NM5 Salary Range: $161 546,00 - $201 932,00 Position purpose: Under the direction of the Secretary-General, acts as the University’s senior adviser, expert, and leader in equity, diversity, and inclusion. Ensures that complaints are processed and that the University policy on student rights and responsible conduct is properly managed. Promotes and maintains a healthy and safe learning environment and an equitable and inclusive workplace. Leverages their expertise and leadership skills to ensure that the University meets its goals in these fields and responds appropriately to various issues. Ensures that the University complies with all current federal and provincial legislation, designs new regulations and procedures, offers expert advice when conflicts arise, and adds to the training and continuing education of members of the University community. In this role, your responsibilities will include: Provides strategic and operational guidance to senior management and University leaders to assist them in taking decisions on issues related to equity and inclusion, particularly in terms of preserving employment equity and preventing harassment, discrimination, and sexual violence, to ensure that the University is well-positioned on these issues. Guides the strategic planning and development of activities related to equity, inclusion, and the prevention of harassment, discrimination, and sexual violence at the University (including regulations, procedures, and practices) in order to foster an inclusive organizational culture and environment that benefits the entire University community (professors, support staff, students, and visitors). Provides advice and expertise to the entire University community (professors, students, support staff) in cases of complaints or conflict, analyzes complex situations, manages complaints, writes reports that comply with applicable laws, and issues recommendations to resolve complaints or conflicts, or proposes other resources. Directs the review of current regulations to ensure that they comply with current legislation, and changes or elaborates regulations and procedures as required. What you will bring: University education in law (LLL, LLB, or JD) in combination with a master’s degree or certification. Over 10 years’ work experience in the field of human rights and/or labour law. Certification or experience in mediation and other dispute resolution methods. Over 10 years’ direct and intensive experience in conflict resolution, including active listening, mediation, and crisis intervention. Appreciable experience (10 years’) in investigatory techniques and in investigating and processing highly confidential, complex complaints involving harassment, discrimination, and sexual violence ( Human Rights Code, Occupational Health and Safety Act), and in seeking positive outcomes. Strong personnel management skills Strong problem-solving and conflict resolution skills Knowledge of university environments Excellent verbal and written communication skills and strong interpersonal skills Self-reliance, diplomacy and tact Bilingualism  *This position may be eligible for a telework arrangement. #LI-Hybrid The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Intermediate-Administrator--Human-Resources_JR39999": {
      "title": "Intermediate Administrator, Human Resources",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Replacement of a temporary position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 12 Job Family: Human Resources Operations # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: HR Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/15 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/26 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 07 Salary Range: $62,027.00 - $78,349.00 About the Faculty of Education: The Faculty of Education, along with its commitment to the promotion, advancement and dissemination of knowledge in the field of education in general, is actively involved in teacher training. Located on the unceded ancestral lands of Algonquin peoples, the Faculty of Education is proud to promote equity, diversity, and inclusion. Moreover, according to the latest QS ranking, our faculty is ranked among the top 100 in the world, and fifth in Canada, for research. Position Purpose Reporting to the Intermediate Generalist, Human Resources , the incumbent performs a variety of activities to support the human resources and payroll operations of the sector while ensuring compliance with related policies and procedures. Assist with other administrative functions (maintenance of office equipment, mail distribution, etc.) as required. In this role, your responsibilities will include: Payroll: Coordinate the payroll process for various employee groups within the sector, monitor payroll workflow, track daily activities, monitor compliance with related policies and implement changes as requested in order to ensure integrity and transparency of the payroll process. Prepare and submit requests for remuneration and other documentation necessary to initiate required action in employee records in order to minimize the possibility of an interruption in the employee’s pay. Verify payroll reports for accuracy and liaise with central Payroll service to reconcile any inconsistencies. Identify and resolve any discrepancies related to payroll information to minimize the possibility of a negative impact on the affected employee. Human resources: Prepare and submit all forms and documentation necessary for the generation of contracts to ensure the continuity in the employee’s pay. Liaise with central Human Resources to stay abreast of any changes in general policies and procedures related to human resources and payroll administration. Coordinate the process for the Work Study program (from posting to cost recovery) to support operational needs of the sector. Provide guidance to managers to facilitate onboarding and develop tools for effective management of work-study hours. Information and client service: Provide information to internal clients on various human resources and payroll processes, deadlines and policies to ensure that these are well understood and properly implemented.  Records management: Maintain an effective filing and records management system to preserve discretion of highly confidential information for personnel of the sector and to ensure that complete and accurate documentation is available for future reference or audit purposes. What you will bring: Knowledge of administration and human resources principles normally acquired through postsecondary studies in a related discipline or an equivalent combination of education and work experience. Minimum 2 years of demonstrated experience in a similar role. Knowledge of computer systems and software including financial systems, spreadsheets, databases, Internet and e-mail. Experience in interpreting, explaining and applying policies and regulations. Experience in a human resources environment. Experience with and understanding of payroll principles. Experience in producing reports. Ability to produce high quality work under pressure while meeting strict deadlines. Strong interpersonal and communication skills. Strong organizational skills. Bilingualism – French and English (spoken and written). #LI-HYBRID #LI-SA1 Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:  Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Intermediate-Software-Developer--Agora-CRM-Project_JR39760": {
      "title": "Intermediate Software Developer, Agora CRM Project",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: New Position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 18 Job Family: Application Development # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: ITS - Engagement and Marketing Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: UOITP Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/15 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/26 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: UOITP Grade 10 Salary Range: $82 450,00 - $102 514,00 Position Purpose: Evaluates user business and software requirements and develops new or enhance existing and off-the-shelf computer applications. Is assigned small projects of limited duration or may participate in a designated portion of a larger project under supervision. Is focused on meeting user needs and improving the efficiency of the University’s operations through system and software solutions. In this role, your responsibilities will include: Analysis : Evaluates client’s business and functional requirements in order to fully explore their needs. Recommends solutions to improve systems and automation. Design: Creates technical designs, specifications or plans in order to translate requirements into technical specification documents. Designs, plans and coordinates effective software using appropriate tools and operating systems facilitating the function of the various departments, to increase their efficiency and productivity, and to reduce costs. Development, Testing and Documentation: Develops effective program code, tests and provides documentation for new releases or application upgrades. Translates specifications document into effective program code. Assists in the provision of user documentation. Creates technical specification, project plans and other documentation for review. Deployment : Installs or releases new software in alignment with established procedures and processes. Integrates the final product to existing software. Maintenance : Reviews and identifies the requirement to repair and maintain current systems and software to ensure they continue to perform in keeping with client expectations. Ensures that all issues concerning data integrity, security, and confidentiality and user secrecy are dealt with. Ensures daily that the systems in production are functional and performing, and that backup and recovery processes and procedures are completed properly. Project Management : Develops and maintains plans outlining steps and timetables for small development projects, including analyzing and estimating feasibility, costs, time and compatibility with hardware and other programs, in order to ensure development projects are completed within an acceptable timeframe and budget and meet client expectations. Training and Advice : Provides specialized advice and training to users on the optimal use of the programs to raise the proficiency of users. Develops and maintains instructional materials for users. Purchasing Advice : Evaluates software options (custom versus off-the-shelf) and measure these against client requirements to offer sound recommendations ensuring the most cost effective and operationally appropriate solutions for user needs. Continuous Improvement : Provides recommendations to improve and enhance existing standards and procedures and improve the quality of the services provided to the users. Data Security: Responsible for ensuring confidentiality, security and accessibility of institutional data. The incumbent complies with and promotes any controls put in place to protect and secure data and exhibits due diligence in following the established information security and privacy policies as well as related standards. What you will bring: University degree in Computer Science or a related field or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Minimum 3 years of experience in the development and implementation of system and software programs. Strong written and verbal communication skills in both official languages (French and English). Knowledge of University policies, guidelines and standards regarding system and software development and deployment. Experience working with Salesforce development languages (such as Apex, LWC and Node.js) ( asset ) or other programming languages (such as PHP and TypeScript). Experience working with databases and query languages such as SOQL, SQL, GraphQL or similar. Experience migrating and transforming data between systems, including data mapping, validation, and error handling. Experience developing and implementing API integrations using REST, JSON, and industry standard authentication mechanisms (e.g. OAuth 2.0). Experience in designing, implementing, and maintaining Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipelines using tools like GitHub, GitLab CI, or Azure DevOps. Analytical skills to diagnose debug and repair programming issues. Ability to communicate technical concepts to users. *This position may be subject to a telework agreement.  #LI-Hybrid Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:  Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
