    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/XMLNAME--2--Tenure-track-faculty-positions-in-the-field-of-Indigenous-Justice_JR40118": {
      "title": "(2) Tenure-track faculty positions in the field of Indigenous Justice",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nJob Type: Academic Regular Faculty/Department: Department of Criminology_FT Department of Criminology Faculty of Social Sciences University of Ottawa   Two (2) tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Assistant Professor in the field of Indigenous Justice   Located on the traditional and unceded territory of the Anishinàbe-Algonquin People, near the Kichi Sìbi river, the Department of Criminology at the University of Ottawa is accepting applications for two (2) tenure-track positions at the rank of Assistant Professor in the field of Indigenous Justice. One (1) of these positions is to teach predominantly in French; the other is linguistically open (English and/or French).   The Faculty of Social Sciences comprises nine departments, schools and institutes, which offer undergraduate, master’s, and doctoral programs in both English and French. With its 10,000 students, 260 full-time professors, and wide array of programs and research centres, the Faculty of Social Sciences plays a key role at the heart of the University of Ottawa. Its graduate students are supervised by excellent researchers and undertake cutting-edge research in the Faculty’s Master’s and Ph.D. programs. The University of Ottawa is committed to creating a supportive environment for Indigenous colleagues and is undertaking cohort hires across the campus to this effect.   In line with a priority of both the Faculty of Social Sciences and the University of Ottawa, candidates shall have in-depth knowledge of Indigenous realities in Canada informed by strong connections with Indigenous communities, a PhD (may be in progress) or a Master’s degree with an extensive background in research, and significant experience in the study of issues affecting Indigenous Peoples and communities from a social science, humanities, education, law, or public health perspective. These positions will contribute to the development of an Indigenous Justice Institute and, as such, the department values candidates with experience in community and social engagement and transformation efforts. It should be noted that the paradigms mobilized by the Department of Criminology are part of critical and/or transformative perspectives in research and intervention. The Department of Criminology currently offers programs in three cycles (BA, MA and Ph.D.) and has joint BA and MA programs with the Institute of Feminist and Gender Studies.   This position is a designated hire restricted to First Nations, Metis, and Inuit (FNMI) candidates. While all qualified individuals are encouraged to apply, priority in selection will be given to candidates who identify as First Nations, Métis, or Inuit. Applicants are invited to identify as First Nations, Metis, and/or Inuit in their cover letter by highlighting their lived experiences, connections to community, and research praxis. Shortlisted candidates will be required to submit documentation according to the university's  Policy 135 for FNMI membership/citizenship verification for all appointments, awards, and funding opportunities for faculty, staff, graduate students and post-doctoral fellows prior to interviewing.    DUTIES Conduct research and publishing activities; Teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels; Supervise Master’s students and Ph.D. students/candidates; Participate in the University’s academic and administrative activities; Carry out other activities as specified in the collective agreement.   QUALIFICATIONS Hold a Ph.D. or equivalent in Criminology or a related field that is relevant to the study of Indigenous Justice (e.g., Indigenous Studies, Sociology, Social Work, Anthropology, Law, Cultural Studies, Political Studies, Humanities, Education, Public Health). Candidates whose Ph.D. is in progress and are close to completion are encouraged to apply and will be considered. As per the APUO Collective Agreement, a Master’s degree in criminology or a related field as well as substantial research experience may be considered equivalent to a doctorate; In-depth knowledge of the history, realities, needs, ancestral and contemporary values and knowledges of Indigenous Peoples and communities; Research, advocacy, or other relevant experience working with Indigenous communities; Experience or demonstrated potential for excellence in teaching and research; Selection will be limited to Indigenous applicants.   BILINGUALISM The University of Ottawa is a bilingual institution (in French and English) and all professors in the Faculty of Social Sciences must be actively bilingual to obtain tenure. Candidates do not have to be actively bilingual at the time of hire. The University of Ottawa offers second-language training to staff members and their spouses.   TERMS AND SALARY Two (2) new tenure-track positions. Salary scale for Assistant Professors starts at $99,377 (as of May 1, 2025).   STARTING DATE July 1st, 2027    LOCATION OF WORK Department of Criminology, University of Ottawa, 120 University, Ottawa, ON, K1N 6N5   BENEFITS PACKAGE The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package , which includes long-term disability insurance, basic group life insurance, health insurance, University of Ottawa pension plan and optional life insurance, as well as reimbursement of eligible relocation expenses.   APPLICATION DEADLINE The deadline for applications is November 9, 2026 ; however, applications may be considered after this date until the positions are filled.   A complete application must include the following elements:   A curriculum vitae; A letter describing their teaching and research experience and interests, experience with Indigenous justice, an example of their current research, and an indication of their French and English language abilities; Copies of their main publications and teaching evaluations.   Applicants must also provide the names and contact information of three referees who may be asked to submit letters of recommendation. As these positions are for Indigenous scholars, applicants who wish to be considered must self-identify in their cover letter (no documentation is required at the time of application).   All information and letters are to be submitted to the following address:   Chris Bruckert Chair, Department of Criminology Faculty of Social Sciences University of Ottawa 120 University, room 14002c Ottawa, Ontario K1N 6N5 Email:  crimino@uottawa.ca   More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online . Apply online through the University of Ottawa Careers portal. In accordance with government policy, all qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada. The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by emailing the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations, at vra.affairesprofessorales@uOttawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The University of Ottawa is proud of its more than 175-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides second-language training to staff members and their spouses."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Manager--Operations_JR39844-1": {
      "title": "Manager, Operations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: New Position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): N/A Job Family: Business Operations # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Administration Office, Faculty of Arts Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: N/A Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/14 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/26 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: Non-Union Grade NM1 Salary Range: $107,503.00 - $134,379.00 About the Faculty of Arts The oldest and one of the biggest faculties at the University of Ottawa. The Faculty of Arts has evolved to be one of the leading faculties on campus. Now more than ever, the new economy needs thinkers, communicators, and creators. Whether you are interested in the Humanities, Languages and Literatures, or Fine and Performing Arts, the Faculty of Arts offers students an education for today's and tomorrow's workplace! The Faculty of Arts is dedicated to creating a diverse, equitable and inclusive environment for everyone. Position purpose:   Reporting to the Chief Administrative Officer , the Manager, Operations, oversees and supports the Lead Officers, Administrative Support within the academic units of the Faculty of Arts, ensuring the efficient management of academic administration, and financial and HR operations. The role collaborates closely with department directors/chairs, and with all other branches of the faculty to align operations with faculty goals, standardize processes, and ensure compliance with institutional policies and regulations.   In this job, your responsibilities will include: Team supervision and leadership: Oversees and supports all Lead Officer, Administrative Support, and their administrative staff as required. Sets objectives, reviews performance with department directors/chairs, and fosters professional development to build a strong, cohesive team. Participates in recruitment, onboarding, disciplinary and performance-related matters as needed. Monitors workload distribution for fairness and efficiency, and ensures consistent training across units, including the development and delivery of a shared training program. Maintains effective communication between administrative staff, department leadership, and faculty support units to ensure alignment and service quality. Provides operational backup support when necessary. Obligations management: Oversees and monitors financial obligations to students across all academic units within the faculty. This includes ensuring that commitments are fulfilled in an accurate and timely manner and in accordance with collective agreements without creating unnecessary or unapproved obligations. To that end, maintains rigorous oversight of financial processes related to student Teaching Assistant contracts and, where appropriate, Research Assistant contracts. The Manager works closely with the Lead Officer, Administrative Support, and central administration to ensure alignment, consistency, and compliance with institutional policies, while fostering a coordinated and efficient approach to managing obligations to students. Financial management: In collaboration with the Manager, Finance, oversees the administration of internally funded budgets, as well as various departmental special project budgets, ensuring compliance with institutional policies. Develops and evaluates business practices and tools to improve financial controls and operational efficiency. Ensures alignment between expenditures and budgets. Monitors and addresses overages and inconsistencies, to ensure compliance with institutional guidelines. Process review and standardization: Leads the review and continuous improvement of administrative processes across academic units to ensure alignment, consistency, and operational efficiency. Develops and implements standardized practices that support equity, reduce duplication, and promote service excellence. Facilitates collaboration and resource sharing between units and ensures that all procedures comply with faculty and university policies, collective agreements, and applicable regulations. Human resources management: In collaboration with the Vice-Dean, Governance and the Faculty’s HR Department, serves as the primary point of contact for academic unit staff on operational matters related to APTPUO and CUPE. Oversees the hiring processes for Teaching Assistants and Part-Time Professors, ensuring alignment with collective agreements and university policies. Reviews and approves contracts as needed, and ensures consistent, timely payroll verification across all academic units. Works proactively with the Payroll Administration employees, to resolve payroll issues and uphold compliance with applicable agreements and legislation. Problem solving and advisement: Acts as a trusted resource and subject matter expert in resolving complex and sensitive operational issues within academic units. Works collaboratively with departmental staff, faculty leadership, and central university services to identify root causes and implement practical, sustainable solutions. Anticipates and addresses recurring challenges by refining systems, streamlining workflows, and embedding long-term improvements that support the academic and administrative mission of the faculty. Academic operations: Ensures the effective and consistent execution of academic administrative processes across all units, supporting course delivery, academic planning, and student services in alignment with faculty and institutional policies. Collaborates with academic leadership and administrative managers to identify and resolve operational challenges that may impact on the academic mission. Consolidation and reporting: Reviews and consolidates departmental reports for the managers of finance and HR as well as the CAO, ensuring accuracy and completeness. Monitors departmental budgets by analyzing historical performance and projecting future needs. Assess staffing levels and operational demands to optimize the use of financial, human and material resources. Follows-up on requests from faculty leadership, professors, students and other stakeholders. Ensures compliance with all administrative services (HR, finance, IT, marketing and facilities) by addressing inconsistencies and implementing corrective actions when required. Collaboration with directors and stakeholders: Works with department directors/chairs to align administrative operations with faculty and departmental goals. Facilitates communication and collaboration across departments, faculty and central university units, including finance, HR, IT marketing and facilities, and central services.   What you will bring: Bachelor’s degree in business administration or a related field; Master’s degree is an asset Minimum of 7 years of administrative experience in an academic setting Minimum of 3 years of supervisory experience, preferably in a unionized environment Strong knowledge of academic processes and governance within post-secondary education Experience in interpreting and applying policies and collective agreements (including OSSTF, CUPE and APTPUO) Demonstrated expertise in administrative management (ex., finance, HR, IT, marketing and facilities) Skills in budget development and financial planning Experience in project management and/or change management, with proven ability to lead process improvement initiatives Strong decision-making and problem-solving skills Ability to streamline operations, improve efficiency and foster collaboration across departments Demonstrated ability to respect diversity and use inclusive behavior, responding with sensitivity and self-awareness to the unique needs of students and colleagues Strong leadership, organizational and planning abilities High emotional intelligence and the ability to manage conflict constructively Excellent interpersonal and teamwork skills and collegiality Bilingualism – French and English (spoken and written) #LI-MB1 #LI-HYBRID Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:  Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Lees-Campus/APTPUO-Hiver-2027-PHT5623-valuation--diagnostic-et-intervention-au-niveau-du-systme-musculo-squelettique_JR40198": {
      "title": "APTPUO-Hiver 2027-PHT5623 Évaluation, diagnostic et intervention au niveau du système musculo-squelettique",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location: 200 Lees - FHS Academic Period: 2027 Winter Semester Faculty: Faculté des sciences de la santé / Faculty of Health Sciences Academic Unit: École des sciences de la réadaptation \\\\ School of Rehabilitation Sciences Course Title: Évaluation, diagnostic et intervention au niveau du système musculo-squelettique Course Code: PHT5623 Section: Course Description: PHT 5623 Évaluation, diagnostic et intervention au niveau du système musculo-squelettique Démarche clinique pour l'évaluation, le diagnostic et l'intervention auprès de personnes ayant des déficiences et limitations d'activités découlant des affections communes du système musculo-squelettique. Posting limited to: Professeur à temps-partiel régulier / Regular Part-Time Professor Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/13 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/13 Expected Enrolment: 25 Approval date: 2026/10/13 Number of credits: 0.25 Work Hours: 6 Hourly Rate: Théorie appliquée / Applied Theory: $111.12 (2024-2025) The academic year starts on September 1 and ends on August 31. These rates do not included vacation pay nor statutory pay. These rates will be applied until a new collective agreement is ratified. Retro will be paid after the ratification. Course type: D Posting type: Régulier / Regular Language of instruction: Français | French Competence in second language: Passive Course Schedule: - - - 26 février Requirements: Diplôme universitaire en physiothérapie; expérience en enseignement universitaire en physiothérapie musculo-squelettique au 2e cycle; connaissances approfondies des approches cliniques en musculo-squelettique; expérience en enseignement universitaire de l’évaluation musculo-squelettique et de la thérapie manuelle, des techniques d’interventions en musculo-squelettiques et en évaluation d’examen pratique en physiothérapie. Heures: 6 (Théorie appliquée) Horaire 26 février Additional Information and/or Comments: An acceptable level of education and/or experience could be viewed as being equivalent to the educational required and/or demonstrated experience. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any adaptive measures you might require. Information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. Employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training. The list of training may be modified by provincial law. The hiring process will be governed by the current APTPUO collective agreements; you can click here for the main unit , here for the OLBI unit , or here for the Toronto/Windsor unit to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point